// packer -- a game's .idml sources, turned into `id` source.
//
// `id` has no file I/O of any kind: stdin and stdout, and nothing else. So a
// packaged game cannot read its assets at runtime, and the only place left to put
// them is the program text. This is the program that puts them there.
//
// In:  every .idml file of a game, concatenated on stdin, each preceded by a
//      line `#file <path>`. That is the compiler's own marker convention and the
//      engine's idml lexer consumes it, so a diagnostic can still name the file
//      an author actually wrote.
// Out: a stream of generated files on stdout, each introduced by a line
//      `@@FILE <relative/path>` followed by that file's bytes. tools/idem splits
//      on the markers and writes them. Which files exist, what they are called
//      and how they nest is decided here, in `id` -- the shell only makes
//      directories and copies bytes.
//
// The generated tree is itself a legal `id` project, which is the whole
// difficulty. Three functions per file and three entries per directory apply to
// generated code exactly as they apply to hand-written code, so the chunks spill
// down a chain of `n/` subdirectories instead of sitting in one flat folder, and
// each chunk function does two things: push its literal, call the next one.

main(int argc, string[] argv) {
  pack_init(argc, argv);
  pack_run();
  flush();
} return int 0;
